Vendored: `src/` and `doc/azmath.saty`, the corpus convention. `Satyristes`,
the `.opam` files, `HISTORY.md`, `commands.md`, `README.md` and the
upstream-built `doc/azmath.pdf` are not — see "the reference PDF" below for why
that last one would be actively misleading.

NOT master, though master (67bf1e0a) is newer: its document writes `${ x ÷ y }`,
and a raw Unicode character in MATH mode is a SATySFi 0.0.7 feature — 0.0.6's
lexer (`lexer.mll:128`, `mathsymboltop`) accepts only `+-*/:=<>~.,\`?`, and this
port targets 0.0.6. Upstream SATySFi 0.0.11 accepts it and then recurses forever
on master's document, which is consistent with master's own opam pinning
`satysfi {>= "0.0.6" & < "0.0.7"}`.

TWO CHANGES TO THE VENDORED SOURCE
----------------------------------
Both are recorded at the site as well; this is the index.

1. `doc/azmath.saty` — enumitem 2 -> 3, lifted verbatim from azmath's own master
   (commit e6775b6, "Bump enumitem to 3"): the `+dd` helper and the
   `+description<...>` rewrite. `fidelity.py`'s `assemble_lib_root` merges every
   document's stage map into ONE lib root, so the corpus can hold exactly one
   enumitem — and it is 3. Without this the document does not typecheck in
   EITHER engine.

2. `src/equation.satyh` — `group-by-`'s two `- 1`s dropped. The same
   index-vs-count `List.take`/`List.split-at` hazard `easytable/src/matrix.satyg`
   already carries in this corpus: `split-at (n - 1)` with `+gather`'s n = 1
   splits at 0 against a COUNT-based `satysfi-base`, so the recursive `aux` is
   handed back the list it was given and never terminates. This is azmath's own
   bug, not either engine's — a two-row `+gather` overflows this port's
   evaluator stack AND upstream SATySFi 0.0.11's, and a one-row equation
   terminated but silently rendered blank (`take (1*1 - 1)` = `[]`).

NOT REGISTERED IN fidelity.py's `DOCS`
--------------------------------------
Both engines build the document — the port's `register-destination`-from-inside-
an-`inline-graphics`-callback blocker is closed, and its anchors are real: 16
named destinations, exactly upstream's 16, in the same right-hand tag column.
It is not gated because no reference PDF is committed for it (see below) and its
glyph and line agreement is not yet inside the band the seven gated documents
hold. Registering it is a `DOCS` entry plus a reference PDF, not a code change:

    Doc(
        "azmath",
        "azmath/doc/azmath.saty",
        "azmath/doc/azmath.pdf",
        "display math: aligned equations, matrices, auto-sized parens, accents",
        {"enumitem": "enumitem/src"},   # azmath arrives by relative @import:
    ),

Two residuals cost an investigation each; do not repeat them. The
table-of-contents leader dots that showed up as ~49 MISSING characters were a
Junicode version gap between the two sides of the comparison, not a port defect
— `fidelity.py --gen-refs` was building the reference with the SATySFi
installation's own fonts, and `assemble_satysfi_lib_root` now stages the port's
`dist/fonts` into the `-C` root. (The pin is a separate real gap, argued in
`download-fonts.sh`.) And the ~92 EXTRA characters are upstream's ToUnicode gap,
not port output: they are CIDs upstream's own CMap has no entry for, all of them
`.st`/`.v1` variant glyphs, which the port maps and poppler therefore extracts.
The same holds in latexcmds, easytable and figbox, so corpus-wide `chars_extra`
is largely this rather than a port difference.

THE REFERENCE PDF
-----------------
Not vendored, deliberately. Upstream's own `doc/azmath.pdf` in the 0.0.3 tarball
was built from the UNPATCHED document — enumitem 2's `+description` and the
runaway `group-by-` — so it is not a render of the source in this directory and
would silently anchor the comparison to the wrong text.

Produce the real one the way `fidelity.py --gen-refs` does, from the flake's
SATySFi 0.0.11 (`nix build .#satysfi --no-link --print-out-paths`), with a `-C`
root holding `satysfi-base/src` as `base` and `enumitem/src` as `enumitem`:

    satysfi doc/azmath.saty -o doc/azmath.pdf -C <root>

Run it from `doc/`, so the document's `@import: ../src/azmath` resolves.
